      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "5849233f-07f3-565c-9c49-72e155c368b7",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Jorge Amado, Sarah Caudwell, Sarah Cockburn\n- Works: The Sirens Sang of Murder, Dona Flor and Her Two Husbands\n- Organizations: Delacorte Press, Random House, Anthony Award\n- Roles: writer, barrister\n- Concepts: Brazilian modernism, detective stories\n\nFacts:\n- Jorge Amado, born Jorge Leal Amado de Faria on 10 August 1912 and died on 6 August 2001, was a Brazilian writer of the modernist school.\n- Sarah Caudwell was the pseudonym of Sarah Cockburn, who was born on 27 May 1939 and died on 28 January 2000, and she was a British barrister and writer of detective stories.\n- The Sirens Sang of Murder was written by Sarah Caudwell and was published by Delacorte Press, a company owned by Random House, on 1 October 1989.\n- The Sirens Sang of Murder won the Anthony Award for Best Novel in 1990.\n- Jorge Amado remained the best known of modern Brazilian writers, and his work was translated into about 49 languages.\n- Jorge Amado's work was adapted into film, including Dona Flor and Her Two Husbands in 1978.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "a8b31a34-a405-5eba-b7ff-c109b0f9f531",
        "text": "Page: Jorge Amado. Sentence: Jorge Leal Amado de Faria (] , 10 August 1912 – 6 August 2001) was a Brazilian writer of the modernist school.\nPage: Jorge Amado. Sentence: He remains the best known of modern Brazilian writers, with his work having been translated into some 49 languages and popularized in film, notably \"Dona Flor and Her Two Husbands\" in 1978.\nPage: Sarah Caudwell. Sentence: Sarah Caudwell was the pseudonym of Sarah Cockburn (27 May 1939 – 28 January 2000), a British barrister and writer of detective stories.\nPage: The Sirens Sang of Murder. Sentence: The Sirens Sang of Murder (ISBN  ) is a book written by Sarah Caudwell and published by Delacorte Press (owned by Random House) on 1 October 1989 which later went on to win the Anthony Award for Best Novel in 1990.",
